# shellcheck shell=bash
# Shared machinery of the slice-0 example gates (PROVISIONAL): slice0_backends.sh (gates 4, 6, 6a)
# and slice0_artifacts.sh (gates 10, 11, 12). Source it from the repository root, inside the nix
# devshell, with `set -euo pipefail`. It builds the compiler and LCB's command line under the
# gate's limits (lib/limits.sh: an address-space cap of SLICE0_GATE_MEM_GB GiB, default 64; 0 for
# a gate whose executables under test need no cap, slice0_artifacts.sh) and defines:
#   capped (a command prefix, an array): run under a 64 GiB address-space cap of its own, so
#     every build and analysis command is capped whatever the gate's cap;
#   root, work (removed on exit), lcb, target, emitter (the Lean emitter command, an array);
#   observe EXE OUT SECS RETRY [MEM_MB [ENV...]]: run EXE under bounded_run (and limited_exec.sh
#     MEM_MB, default 1024) with ENV assignments; OUT.status (status or "incomplete"),
#     OUT.stdout, OUT.stderr;
#   judge EXPECTED_DIR OBS: the observation OBS.* equals the expectation, byte for byte;
#   build NAME LOG CMD...: run CMD, or print LOG and fail the gate;
#   emit_examples: the example names, one per line, failing if there are none;
#   cap_out: copy stdin to stdout, failing above 1 MiB: every command output a gate captures
#     into a variable goes through it, so no producer can grow the gate's shell (which may run
#     with no address-space cap, see SLICE0_GATE_MEM_GB) without bound.
root=$(pwd)
work=$(mktemp -d)
trap 'rm -rf "${work:?}"' EXIT
BOUNDED_PARENT="$work"
# shellcheck source=scripts/checks/lib/bounded.sh
source scripts/checks/lib/bounded.sh
[ "$BOUNDED_HAVE_WATCHDOG" = 1 ] || { echo "slice0: python3 is required for bounded runs" >&2; exit 2; }
# shellcheck source=scripts/checks/lib/limits.sh
source scripts/checks/lib/limits.sh
gate_limits "${SLICE0_GATE_MEM_GB:-64}" 40000
capped=(bash "$root/scripts/checks/lib/limited_exec.sh" 65536)
"${capped[@]}" timeout -k 30 1800 lake build Concrete lean-compiler-backend/lcb >/dev/null
lcb="$root/.lake/packages/lean-compiler-backend/.lake/build/bin/lcb"
case "$(uname -s)-$(uname -m)" in
  Linux-x86_64) target=x86_64-linux ;;
  Linux-aarch64) target=aarch64-linux ;;
  Darwin-arm64) target=aarch64-macos ;;
  *) echo "slice0: unsupported host $(uname -s)-$(uname -m)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac
emitter=("${capped[@]}" timeout -k 10 120 lake env lean --run scripts/checks/slice0_bir.lean)

observe() {
  local rc=0 mem=${5:-1024}
  local envs=("${@:6}")
  bounded_run -i /dev/null -r "$4" "$3" -- env ${envs[@]+"${envs[@]}"} \
    bash "$root/scripts/checks/lib/limited_exec.sh" "$mem" "$1" \
    >"$2.stdout" 2>"$2.stderr" || rc=$?
  if bounded_is_incomplete "$rc"; then echo incomplete >"$2.status"; else echo "$rc" >"$2.status"; fi
}

judge() {
  cmp -s "$1/status" "$2.status" && cmp -s "$1/stderr" "$2.stderr" && [ ! -s "$2.stdout" ]
}

build() {
  local name=$1 log=$2; shift 2
  "$@" >"$log" 2>&1 || { cat "$log" >&2; echo "slice0: $name: build failed: $*" >&2; exit 1; }
}

cap_out() {
  local LC_ALL=C out
  out=$(head -c 1048577; echo x)
  out=${out%x}
  [ "${#out}" -le 1048576 ] || { echo "slice0: captured output over 1 MiB" >&2; return 1; }
  printf '%s' "$out"
}

emit_examples() {
  local names
  names=$("${emitter[@]}" list | cap_out) || { echo "slice0: listing the examples failed" >&2; exit 1; }
  [ -n "$names" ] || { echo "slice0: no examples listed" >&2; exit 1; }
  printf '%s\n' "$names"
}
7:This document describes **the compiler**: its stages, IRs, proof strategy, premises and gates. Language rules are not restated here. They live in `ConcreteSpec`, and this document links to them:
17:Each arrow is a total Lean function of the compiler. **Proved** means a kernel-checked theorem. **Premise** means a field of one of the two premise structures (frontend, native) in the one file `Concrete/Premises.lean`.
22:  ▼                                   until done: premise P_lex (implementation)
25:  ▼                                   premise P_parse (implementation) until the generic theorem lands
48:  ▼ (unverified: premise P_print)              ▼ (first backend proof target: adapter_correct
49:.ll text  (`out.ll`)                           │  against LCB's imported semantics; P_adapter until proved)
51:  │ (premise P_toolchain[llvm])                │ to LCB IN PROCESS (no text on this path);
53:  ▼                                            ▼ (premise P_toolchain[lcb]: LCB's named trust boundaries)
55:  │ run: premises P_launch, P_live (environment), under the target profile
107:The **LLVM printer** is unverified and stays a premise (`P_print`) until an LLVM-IR semantics exists.
109:The **BIR → LCB-IR adapter** is the first backend proof target. `adapter_correct` states that the adapter's output, under **LCB's own semantics** (`LeanCompilerBackend.Verified.Source`, the `ssa-int-v2` profile, imported and never restated), has the BIR behaviour. It is proved (`Proof.adapter_correct`) for every WF program the adapter accepts (`adapt p = .ok f`; the adapter refuses the rest, so no artifact exists for them). It supplies semantic preservation for successful adaptation; `P_adapter` stays in the premise list until the backend contract is resolved: `lcb`'s `emit` must represent adaptation failure (`adapt` returns `Except String Source.Function`) or the contract must be restricted to accepted programs, and the theorem composed into `NativePremises lcb`. The trusted-base golden (gate 9) then records the retirement.
140:A fixed runtime entry, `Backend/Runtime.lean`, is emitted per backend as LLVM text and as QBE text for LCB's command line (the runtime is not on the adapter's proof path; it is premise `P_rt`). It calls `concrete_body`, and then:
144:The decoding lives in this one place for both backends. Its correctness is premise `P_rt` (implementation, over libc `write`/`_exit`): an image whose `concrete_body` returns word `w` has the behaviour `decode w`, and a body that diverges gives a run that diverges.
161:- The gates and runners (conformance, the two differentials, certificates, the premise registry, negative controls) are files in the existing `scripts/checks/`. No `scripts/compiler/` is added.
